  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAs of 18z, water vapor imagery and H5 objective analysis shows \nan expanding mid-level ridge across the south-central and \nsoutheast CONUS, with deep troughing extending south from \nnorthwest Canada into the Four Corners region. At the surface, \nsprawling high pressure across the southeast CONUS is keeping\nconditions dry and very warm across the local area. \n\nAs the H5 ridging and southwest surface flow intensify tonight\ninto Tuesday, tropical moisture from both the eastern Pacific\n(specifically Hurricane Simon) and the western Gulf/Bay of\nCampeche, will be advected north and east to the region. Under\nthe ridge, 850 mb temperatures will rise to 18-20C (near or\nexceeding climatological values for mid-October), yielding high\ntemperatures in the lower to middle 90s (also near or exceeding\nrecord highs).\n\nAdditionally, the infusion of tropical moisture will bring dew \npoint temperatures into the upper 60s to lower 70s. Putting it \nall together, extremely anomalous heat index values in the \nupper 90s and low 100s are expected over the western half of the\nregion. These heat index will also approach or exceed monthly \nrecord high values for October. While short of Heat Advisory \ncriteria, we will be issuing a Special Weather Statement for \nsoutheast Missouri, the KY Purchase, and southwest Illinois for \nheat impacts on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nWednesday and Thursday, the H5 ridge will become suppressed\nslightly as a baroclinic zone develops just north and west of\nthe area. Weak shortwaves within this flow will lead to the\ndevelopment of scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms\nWednesday into Thursday morning across most of the area.\nMoisture will pool along and south of the baroclinic zone, and \nPWAT values will be as high as 300-350% of normal. So while \nclouds and rain will keep temperatures down slightly, dew point\ntemperatures will be near record high values for October, in \nthe middle to upper 70s! \n\nAt this time, the potential for severe weather looks pretty \nminimal. Per the REFS, only a small part of the area in \nsoutheast Missouri and southwest Illinois has more than a 10% \nchance having SBCAPE over 500 J/kg and over 30 kts of 0-6 km \nshear present. However, locally heavy rainfall is possible with \nthe elevated PWAT values. Right now, most of the area is \nforecast to see about 0.25 to 0.75\" of rain by Thursday evening,\nfrom southeast to northwest, respectively. We may see a bit of a\nlull in activity Thursday afternoon as the ridge briefly expands\nbefore a deep H5 trough ushers a strong cold front through the \narea Thursday night into Friday morning. \n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front, breezy and much cooler\nconditions are forecast Friday through next weekend. High \npressure will build south from the northern Great Plains, \nbringing dry and seasonably cool temperatures for this weekend. \nForecast low temperatures in the middle to upper 30s Sunday \nmorning could even bring our first widespread frost of the \nseason.",
